WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.308 --> 00:00:01.258
- Good morning, Believe Nation!

00:00:01.258 --> 00:00:02.581
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.581 --> 00:00:03.828
My one word, is believe.

00:00:03.828 --> 00:00:05.747
And I believe that
entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:05.747 --> 00:00:07.738
all of the major problems, of the world.

00:00:07.738 --> 00:00:11.337
So, today's message is,
get used to rejection!

00:00:11.337 --> 00:00:12.989
Over to you, Jack Ma.

00:00:12.989 --> 00:00:15.277
(rooster crowing)

00:00:15.277 --> 00:00:17.786
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:17.786 --> 00:00:20.465
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:24.728
♫ I wake up every mornin' ♫

00:00:24.728 --> 00:00:28.435
- When I, before I
graduate from universities,

00:00:28.435 --> 00:00:30.542
and before, for three years,

00:00:30.542 --> 00:00:32.501
I tried to fill in the universities.

00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:36.418
So, I applied jobs, for
30 times, got rejected.

00:00:37.421 --> 00:00:38.750
I went for police.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.259
They said no, you're not good.

00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:42.909
I went to, even the KFC.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:44.709
When KFC come to China, come to my city...

00:00:44.709 --> 00:00:46.357
(audience laughing)

00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:47.347
- [Interviewer] You tried.

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:49.764
- 24 people went for the job.

00:00:50.807 --> 00:00:52.566
23 people accepted.

00:00:52.566 --> 00:00:54.966
I was the only guy.
(audience laughing)

00:00:54.966 --> 00:00:56.266
And we went for police!

00:00:56.266 --> 00:00:58.568
Five people, four of them accepted!

00:00:58.568 --> 00:01:00.977
I was the only the guy,
that did not receive it.

00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:04.216
So, to me, being turned down, rejected,

00:01:04.216 --> 00:01:07.594
oh, by the way, I told you
that I applied for Harvard,

00:01:07.594 --> 00:01:08.427
for ten times.

00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:10.392
Rejected.
(audience laughing)

00:01:10.392 --> 00:01:12.183
I know, how to be rejected!

00:01:12.183 --> 00:01:14.821
- [Interviewer] They're sorry now.

00:01:14.821 --> 00:01:16.421
Ten times, you wrote them, and said,

00:01:16.421 --> 00:01:18.213
I'd like to come to Harvard.

00:01:18.213 --> 00:01:19.046
- Yeah.

00:01:19.046 --> 00:01:19.879
(clears throat) And then, I told myself,

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.511
someday, I should go teach there.

00:01:21.511 --> 00:01:23.949
(audience laughing)

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.402
- So, I never applied to KFC, or Harvard,

00:01:26.402 --> 00:01:28.210
or got rejected from those places.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.989
But, I think rejection is part
of life, as an entrepreneur.

00:01:31.989 --> 00:01:34.741
You are going to get rejected
over, and over, and over,

00:01:34.741 --> 00:01:35.612
and over again.

00:01:35.612 --> 00:01:38.061
It's just part of being an entrepreneur,

00:01:38.061 --> 00:01:39.231
and being in business.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:41.129
The first thing you put
out, won't be very good.

00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:42.000
People will hate on it.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.531
People will give some
constructive criticism as well.

00:01:44.531 --> 00:01:46.232
People will say no, because
they don't know you,

00:01:46.232 --> 00:01:47.370
trust you, like you.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.870
And so, you won't get those first deals.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.388
It takes a lot of time,

00:01:50.388 --> 00:01:52.188
to build up a business,
and make is successful.

00:01:52.188 --> 00:01:53.926
And even when you've gotten to that point,

00:01:53.926 --> 00:01:56.138
where you're feeling happy,
and you're bringing on a team,

00:01:56.138 --> 00:01:57.577
and building the business,

00:01:57.577 --> 00:02:00.085
you're still going to
get rejected, constantly.

00:02:00.085 --> 00:02:02.338
It's just part of life,
as an entrepreneur.

00:02:02.338 --> 00:02:04.389
And so, if you can't handle that,

00:02:04.389 --> 00:02:05.876
it's going to be a really hard road,

00:02:05.876 --> 00:02:07.823
for you to be an entrepreneur.

00:02:07.823 --> 00:02:09.295
I think, the way to get through rejection,

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:10.444
there's two ways to do it.

00:02:10.444 --> 00:02:12.354
One, is to use rejection as fuel,

00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:13.794
kind of like what Jack Ma did.

00:02:13.794 --> 00:02:16.005
I got rejected, ten times, by Harvard.

00:02:16.005 --> 00:02:18.046
One day, I'm going to
go teach there, right?

00:02:18.046 --> 00:02:19.674
One day, I'm going to go speak there.

00:02:19.674 --> 00:02:21.784
To use that as fuel,
that you turned me down,

00:02:21.784 --> 00:02:23.154
I'm going to show you!

00:02:23.154 --> 00:02:24.295
I'm going to beat it!

00:02:24.295 --> 00:02:27.023
I'm going to come back, and
be the teacher there, right?

00:02:27.023 --> 00:02:28.123
You turn me down, for this deal,

00:02:28.123 --> 00:02:30.854
I'm going to get a deal ten times as big!

00:02:30.854 --> 00:02:31.853
Right, so that's one.

00:02:31.853 --> 00:02:34.073
You can use rejection, as fuel,

00:02:34.073 --> 00:02:36.233
to help you go off and
do something bigger,

00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:39.241
and motivation to, I'm
going to prove them wrong.

00:02:39.241 --> 00:02:42.395
The second way, is just
to forget about it,

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:43.744
just get used to it.

00:02:43.744 --> 00:02:44.644
That's more what I do.

00:02:44.644 --> 00:02:46.464
I don't really use rejection as fuel.

00:02:46.464 --> 00:02:48.420
It doesn't really bother me,
when somebody turns me down,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.120
or when a new series doesn't work.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.610
I was tryin' to think, when I
was preparin' for this video,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.100
what's a recent rejection that I have?

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.640
'Cause, there's been so many,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.988
but none really stick out,
as being super memorable.

00:03:00.988 --> 00:03:01.928
The one that I came up with,

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:04.509
was I tried a new series on the weekend,

00:03:04.509 --> 00:03:07.339
a while ago, about the
Best of Kickstarter,

00:03:07.339 --> 00:03:08.900
and it was a new series
that I was testing,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.778
of showing different Kickstarter ideas,

00:03:10.778 --> 00:03:12.459
that I thought, might be helpful for you.

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:14.908
And that video didn't do as
well as I would've liked,

00:03:14.908 --> 00:03:16.789
didn't do as well as other videos,

00:03:16.789 --> 00:03:19.169
and will probably going
to kill that series.

00:03:19.169 --> 00:03:20.409
But, that's okay.

00:03:20.409 --> 00:03:22.435
I've killed tons of series.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.124
It happens all the time, on the channel,

00:03:24.124 --> 00:03:26.103
and I couldn't even
tell you all the series

00:03:26.103 --> 00:03:28.443
that I've killed, because
they didn't perform.

00:03:28.443 --> 00:03:30.332
You guys rejected them.

00:03:30.332 --> 00:03:31.363
It's okay.

00:03:31.363 --> 00:03:32.855
I just moved passed it.

00:03:32.855 --> 00:03:35.043
And so, either one.

00:03:35.043 --> 00:03:36.993
You have two strategies, to go for.

00:03:36.993 --> 00:03:39.353
Use the rejection, as fuel, to say,

00:03:39.353 --> 00:03:43.520
I'm going to show you, how wrong
you are, and prove it to them!

00:03:44.882 --> 00:03:47.463
Or, just forget about it, get used to it,

00:03:47.463 --> 00:03:49.352
it's going to happen,
just part of the game.

00:03:49.352 --> 00:03:50.185
And you move on.

00:03:50.185 --> 00:03:51.166
Just think about the next thing

00:03:51.166 --> 00:03:52.455
that you can go off and build.

00:03:52.455 --> 00:03:53.506
Use either one of those strategies,

00:03:53.506 --> 00:03:55.526
but don't get stuck in the rejection.

00:03:55.526 --> 00:03:57.215
I think that's the most important part.

00:03:57.215 --> 00:03:58.785
If you get stuck, and
you're sitting at home,

00:03:58.785 --> 00:04:01.011
or in your office, like,
this is never going to work!

00:04:01.011 --> 00:04:02.030
You said, no!

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.241
And you just sit there,

00:04:03.241 --> 00:04:05.081
and you don't take any kind of action,

00:04:05.081 --> 00:04:06.822
that's where it really hurts you.

00:04:06.822 --> 00:04:09.182
So, the question of the
day, today, is I'm curious,

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:11.341
how do you deal with rejection?

00:04:11.341 --> 00:04:12.862
Do you use it as fuel?

00:04:12.862 --> 00:04:14.372
Do you just forget about it, and move on?

00:04:14.372 --> 00:04:16.612
Do you do somethin' else,
that I haven't heard of yet?

00:04:16.612 --> 00:04:17.445
Let me know!

00:04:17.445 --> 00:04:18.322
Leave it down, in the comments!

00:04:18.322 --> 00:04:19.741
I'm really excited to find out.

00:04:19.741 --> 00:04:21.822
I also want to give a quick
shout out, to Kaleb Autman!

00:04:21.822 --> 00:04:23.001
Kaleb, thank you so much,

00:04:23.001 --> 00:04:25.581
for pickin' up a copy of
my book, Your One Word.

00:04:25.581 --> 00:04:26.992
It really, really means a lot to me,

00:04:26.992 --> 00:04:29.041
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:04:29.041 --> 00:04:30.472
So, thank you guys, again, for watching.

00:04:30.472 --> 00:04:31.691
I believe in you!

00:04:31.691 --> 00:04:33.580
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.833
and whatever your one word is!

00:04:35.833 --> 00:04:37.203
And I'll see you again, tomorrow morning,

00:04:37.203 --> 00:04:39.867
for another shot of #Entspresso.

00:04:39.867 --> 00:04:42.395
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:04:42.395 --> 00:04:46.562
♫ Espresso keep me goin' ♫

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:51.004
- Let me tell you,
rejection is the big kahuna,

00:04:51.004 --> 00:04:52.783
in all entrepreneurship.

00:04:52.783 --> 00:04:54.823
Show me somebody, who can take a hit,

00:04:54.823 --> 00:04:56.603
and I'll show you a good entrepreneur.

00:04:56.603 --> 00:04:59.867
I don't know if I made
that up, but I think I did.

00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:03.887
But, it's all in how well you get back up.

00:05:03.887 --> 00:05:05.597
And you know what I
have found, by the way?

00:05:05.597 --> 00:05:07.167
Because, if you think about it,

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:09.817
great salespeople make a
couple million dollars a year,

00:05:09.817 --> 00:05:11.797
selling apartments, in New York City,

00:05:11.797 --> 00:05:13.887
which my great salespeople do.

00:05:13.887 --> 00:05:15.393
Great entrepreneurs.

00:05:15.393 --> 00:05:16.612
All business comes from them.

00:05:16.612 --> 00:05:17.631
They manage their own business.

00:05:17.631 --> 00:05:18.481
They hire staff.

00:05:18.481 --> 00:05:19.532
They're entrepreneurs.

00:05:19.532 --> 00:05:21.352
And you know what I found,
is the only difference,

00:05:21.352 --> 00:05:24.692
between the superstars,
making the millions a year,

00:05:24.692 --> 00:05:26.263
and the person, who's barely getting by?

00:05:26.263 --> 00:05:29.571
The only difference, not
contacts, not how hard they work.

00:05:29.571 --> 00:05:32.241
Some people work just as hard,
and don't make any money.

00:05:32.241 --> 00:05:34.822
It's how long they feel
sorry for themselves,

00:05:34.822 --> 00:05:36.322
once they get hit.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.560
Some people could take a hit,
and milk it for two weeks.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:41.451
They're at their desks.

00:05:41.451 --> 00:05:43.231
They look like they're working,
but they're not working.

00:05:43.231 --> 00:05:44.792
Mentally, they're not there.

00:05:44.792 --> 00:05:47.087
The great salespeople take the hit,

00:05:47.087 --> 00:05:49.390
and then, they jump right back up,

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:50.890
as though, they've been hurt,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:52.407
just as much as the next guy.

00:05:52.407 --> 00:05:54.469
But, they work right
through it, and keep goin'.

00:05:54.469 --> 00:05:56.060
Let me tell ya, if you have that skill,

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.128
you should be an entrepreneur,

00:05:57.128 --> 00:05:59.090
'cause you'll be paid well for it.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:02.439
The single best thing I do,
more than anything else,

00:06:02.439 --> 00:06:04.869
I can do, in building
businesses, for myself,

00:06:04.869 --> 00:06:07.919
and these other people, while
just speaking for myself,

00:06:07.919 --> 00:06:09.169
is I fail well.

00:06:10.018 --> 00:06:13.612
If I fall on my face, let me
tell you, nobody's better,

00:06:13.612 --> 00:06:16.989
at pretending I'm not
thinking of a quick cover up,

00:06:16.989 --> 00:06:19.639
tryin' to keep movin',
and you want to know,

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:21.950
in that act itself, of
getting back up fast,

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.521
you always stumble onto something good.

00:06:24.521 --> 00:06:26.051
And you want to know, one other thing,

00:06:26.051 --> 00:06:27.659
this is, I'm not a religious person,

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:29.171
'cause I was raised so goddamned, really,

00:06:29.171 --> 00:06:30.918
just, I don't want to
ever think about it again.

00:06:30.918 --> 00:06:33.086
But, I do believe, once you put your mind,

00:06:33.086 --> 00:06:34.816
and going forward on anything,

00:06:34.816 --> 00:06:36.845
there's almost like a battle cry,

00:06:36.845 --> 00:06:38.064
that goes out in the universe,

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:40.760
and the world rallies 'round you.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:41.593
- You're right.

00:06:41.593 --> 00:06:44.052
- Just comes to you for, again and again.

00:06:44.052 --> 00:06:45.320
So, to have that kind of good faith,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:46.380
that I'm just gettin' back up,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.665
even if I'm going to get slammed,

00:06:48.665 --> 00:06:50.923
life is fair, in its own
weird way, in that way,

00:06:50.923 --> 00:06:52.209
in building businesses.

00:06:52.209 --> 00:06:54.618
You will be swept up,
by some lucky chance.

00:06:54.618 --> 00:06:57.567
It happened to me, every
time I got back up.

00:06:57.567 --> 00:06:58.400
- A little while later,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.098
I wrote a book called, The Shining.

00:07:00.098 --> 00:07:01.619
And it was my third book.

00:07:01.619 --> 00:07:04.658
And I got contractual rights,

00:07:04.658 --> 00:07:08.257
to write the first draft
screenplay, for that,

00:07:08.257 --> 00:07:09.508
and I did do that.

00:07:09.508 --> 00:07:12.527
And I found out later,
that Stanley Kubrick,

00:07:12.527 --> 00:07:15.167
who had got the rights,
through Warner Brothers,

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:16.575
had already determined that he,

00:07:16.575 --> 00:07:18.924
and a lady, named Diane Johnson,

00:07:18.924 --> 00:07:22.813
were going to do the
screenplay, for The Shining.

00:07:22.813 --> 00:07:25.953
So, they basically, my screenplay went in,

00:07:25.953 --> 00:07:27.344
and they said, "No, this won't do."

00:07:27.344 --> 00:07:29.994
And then, they went on, to
what they really wanted to do,

00:07:29.994 --> 00:07:30.844
which was fine.

00:07:30.844 --> 00:07:33.114
I wasn't angry, or upset.

00:07:33.114 --> 00:07:34.733
You're not, after a while.

00:07:34.733 --> 00:07:36.664
You get thousands of rejection slips,

00:07:36.664 --> 00:07:38.434
before you break through.

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:40.045
You kind of get used to that.

00:07:40.045 --> 00:07:42.564
And it's just part of the game.

00:07:42.564 --> 00:07:44.213
But, the thing is, you learn.

00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:45.444
You learn, little by little.

00:07:45.444 --> 00:07:48.527
(upbeat jazzy music)

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.294
- You know, yes.

00:07:52.294 --> 00:07:53.484
The answer, is yes.

00:07:53.484 --> 00:07:55.048
You always get a lot of rejection,

00:07:55.048 --> 00:07:58.029
any time you're going to put
something new into the world.

00:07:58.029 --> 00:08:00.600
There's a million people,
who will tell you,

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.130
how they'd done it better before,

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:06.928
or how that's already been done,
or how it's not going to work.

00:08:06.928 --> 00:08:09.847
And you have to look at
those, as motivators,

00:08:09.847 --> 00:08:11.467
just like, if you're going into acting,

00:08:11.467 --> 00:08:13.966
you can't look at auditioning,
as a way of getting a job.

00:08:13.966 --> 00:08:16.268
You have to look at
auditioning, as the job,

00:08:16.268 --> 00:08:17.295
because that's what you're going to spend

00:08:17.295 --> 00:08:18.865
the majority of your time doing.

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:22.325
And if you love it,
getting the job is gravy.

00:08:22.325 --> 00:08:23.158
So, sure!

00:08:24.481 --> 00:08:28.335
The trickiest thing,
is continuing to work.

00:08:28.335 --> 00:08:31.508
And I was very lucky, with
Heights, that I had Tommy Kail,

00:08:31.508 --> 00:08:33.778
because I had someone,
who wasn't me, saying,

00:08:33.778 --> 00:08:35.508
"Bring in something Friday.

00:08:35.508 --> 00:08:37.058
"Bring in something, on Friday.

00:08:37.058 --> 00:08:37.891
"This is good.

00:08:37.891 --> 00:08:39.319
"This part feels weird.

00:08:39.319 --> 00:08:41.139
"Bring in another song, on Friday.

00:08:41.139 --> 00:08:42.654
"Every week, for a year."

